EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     The following KKyyuuaaffiillee is the simplest you can define.  It provides a
     test suite definition and registers a couple of different test programs
     using different interfaces:

           syntax(2)

           test_suite('first')

           atf_test_program{name='integration_test'}
           plain_test_program{name='legacy_test'}

     The following example is a bit more elaborate.  It introduces some meta‐
     data properties to the test program definitions and recurses into a cou‐
     ple of subdirectories:

           syntax(2)

           test_suite('second')

           plain_test_program{name='legacy_test',
                              allowed_architectures='amd64 i386',
                              required_files='/bin/ls',
                              timeout=30}

           tap_test_program{name='privileged_test',
                            required_user='root'}

           include('module-1/Kyuafile')
           include('module-2/Kyuafile')

     The syntax to define custom properties may be not obvious if their names
     have any characters that make the property name not be a valid Lua iden‐
     tifier.  Dashes are just one example.  To set such properties, do some‐
     thing like this:

           syntax(2)

           test_suite('FreeBSD')

           plain_test_program{name='the_test',
                              ['custom.FreeBSD-Bug-Id']='category/12345'}

   FFrreeeeBBSSDD jjaaiill eexxeeccuuttiioonn eennvviirroonnmmeenntt
     The following example configures the test to be run within a temporary
     jail with vnet(9) support and the permission to create raw sockets:

           syntax(2)

           test_suite('FreeBSD')

           atf_test_program{name='network_test',
                            execenv='jail',
                            execenv_jail_params='vnet allow.raw_sockets',
                            required_user='root'}

     A test case itself may have no requirements in superuser privileges, but
     required_user='root' metadata property reminds that the jail execution
     environment requires kyua(1) being running with root privileges, and the
     test is skipped otherwise with the respective message.  The combination
     of _e_x_e_c_e_n_v set to ‘jail’ and _r_e_q_u_i_r_e_d___u_s_e_r set to ‘unprivileged’ does not
     work respectively.

   CCoonnnneeccttiinngg ddiissjjooiinntt tteesstt ssuuiitteess
     Now suppose you had various test suites on your file system and you would
     like to connect them together so that they could be executed and treated
     as a single unit.  The test suites we would like to connect live under
     _/_u_s_r_/_t_e_s_t_s, _/_u_s_r_/_l_o_c_a_l_/_t_e_s_t_s and _~_/_l_o_c_a_l_/_t_e_s_t_s.

     We cannot create a KKyyuuaaffiillee that references these because the iinncclluuddee()
     directive does not support absolute paths.  Instead, what we can do is
     create a shadow tree using symbolic links:

           $ mkdir ~/everything
           $ ln -s /usr/tests ~/everything/system-tests
           $ ln -s /usr/local/tests ~/everything/local-tests
           $ ln -s ~/local/tests ~/everything/home-tests

     And then we create an _~_/_e_v_e_r_y_t_h_i_n_g_/_K_y_u_a_f_i_l_e file to drive the execution
     of the integrated test suite:

           syntax(2)

           test_suite('test-all-the-things')

           include('system-tests/Kyuafile')
           include('local-tests/Kyuafile')
           include('home-tests/Kyuafile')

     Or, simply, you could reuse the sample top-level KKyyuuaaffiillee to avoid having
     to manually craft the list of directories into which to recurse:

           $ cp /usr/share/examples/kyua/Kyuafile.top ~/everything/Kyuafile

